.TH WHYTOP 1 "2026-09-21" "whytop" "System Administration Utilities"
.SH NAME
whytop \- live terminal UI for troubleshooting Linux servers
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B whytop
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B whytop
is a process monitor for working out why a machine is behaving badly, rather
than only what it is doing. Alongside the process table it shows per-core CPU,
memory, swap, disk and network meters, and a verdict line naming the problems
it can see: a full filesystem, a failed unit, a process the OOM killer
recently killed, a mount that has stopped responding.
.PP
Opening a process shows its tree, its open files and sockets, the systemd unit
that started it, and the tail of that unit's journal, refreshed live.
.PP
.B whytop
watches the Linux machine it is installed on, and only that machine: to
watch another server, log in to it and run
.B whytop
there.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-i " duration"
Sampling interval. Values below 500ms are raised to 500ms. Default 2s.
.TP
.BI \-pid " n"
Open the panel for this PID on startup.
.TP
.BI \-port " n"
Open the panel for the process listening on this TCP port.
.TP
.B \-no\-update\-check
Do not check whether a newer release exists. See
.BR UPDATES .
.TP
.B \-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH KEYS
The footer lists the keys that do something on the current screen;
.B h
shows the full map. Where
.BR htop (1)
and
.BR top (1)
have a key for something,
.B whytop
uses theirs.
.TP
.B Up Down PgUp PgDn Home End
Move the cursor.
.TP
.B k
Kill the selected process with SIGTERM, after a confirmation.
.TP
.B K
Show or hide kernel threads.
.TP
.B P M T
Sort by CPU, memory or time.
.TP
.B < >
Previous or next sort column.
.TP
.B t
Tree view. The selected process's own subtree is coloured, so it is clear
which PIDs belong to it.
.TP
.B /
Search. Tab changes which column it searches.
.TP
.B m
Hand the mouse to the terminal, so text can be selected and copied
normally, with the terminal's own selection and right-click menu. A
right-click anywhere in
.B whytop
does the same thing, since that is the gesture people reach for when they
want to copy. Press
.B m
again to return the mouse to
.BR whytop ,
where clicks select rows and sort columns and the wheel scrolls. While the
terminal has it, the footer says so.
.TP
.B Enter
Open the selected process's panel. In the panel,
.B f
pauses or resumes the live journal,
.B x
and
.B X
stop or force kill,
.B r
restarts the unit, and
.B e
edits its unit file.
.SH UPDATES
.B whytop
checks at most once every twelve hours whether a newer release has been
published, and asks before doing anything. It is the only network connection
.B whytop
makes.
.PP
.B A copy installed from a distribution package never replaces itself.
It reports that a newer version exists and names the package manager command
instead, because a package must not modify files outside the package manager's
knowledge. Packages built for Debian have the check disabled entirely at build
time; see
.IR /usr/share/doc/whytop/README.Debian .
.PP
Setting
.BR WHYTOP_NO_UPDATE_CHECK ,
.B NO_UPDATE_CHECK
or
.B DO_NOT_TRACK
in the environment disables the check, as does
.BR \-no\-update\-check .
.SH PRIVILEGES
.B whytop
runs usefully as an unprivileged user, but other users' sockets, disk I/O and
open files are only visible to root. Rather than running it as root, the
capabilities it actually needs can be granted to the binary:
.PP
.RS
.nf
setcap cap_sys_ptrace,cap_dac_read_search,cap_sys_admin+ep /usr/bin/whytop
.fi
.RE
.PP
Every privileged action \(em signals, unit restarts,
.BR daemon\-reload ,
emptying a file, closing a descriptor \(em is logged to syslog under
.B AUTHPRIV
naming the
.B SUDO_USER
responsible. Read\-only browsing is never logged.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B WHYTOP_NO_UPDATE_CHECK
Disable the update check when set to anything other than
.I 0
or
.IR false .
.TP
.B NO_UPDATE_CHECK
Honoured the same way.
.TP
.B DO_NOT_TRACK
Honoured the same way.
.TP
.B SUDO_USER
Recorded in the audit log for every privileged action.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/whytop/update.json
Records an update the operator declined or postponed. Absent until then.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Exited normally.
.TP
.B 1
Could not start \(em for example, run on a system other than Linux.
.SH EXAMPLES
Watch this machine, sampling once a second:
.PP
.RS
.nf
whytop \-i 1s
.fi
.RE
.PP
Open whatever is listening on port 443:
.PP
.RS
.nf
whytop \-port 443
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR htop (1),
.BR top (1),
.BR iotop (8),
.BR journalctl (1),
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR ss (8)
